course: everyday
lesson: 371
title: Walking away
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On the corner of Station Road a boy stepped right up to Sam, stuck his chin out and said «go on then, hit me». Sam's chest burned, because walking away looks like losing. He walked away anyway. The boy shouted «chicken!» three times, then four, then found he was shouting at an empty pavement, and went home for his tea. A fight needs two people; Sam simply did not turn up to it.
explain: Jake, who fights about everything, heard a boy call his football team rubbish. His fists itched and his face went hot. For the first time in his life he counted to ten, turned round and left. The boy was left shouting at nobody and looked silly, Jake's mates were amazed, and Jake did not get a detention for once that term.
explain: Walking away is not only leaving trouble, it is walking towards safety. When older boys followed Sam and Freddie, they did not run into an empty alley; they walked calmly into the newsagent's. The shopkeeper let them stay until the boys had gone and rang Freddie's mum. Lights, shops and grown-ups are the right direction.
explain: Others tried it too. When an older girl pushed Maddie at the park, Maddie remembered her sore nose from last time and walked home, annoyed with herself and proud at the same time; her mum said it was the most grown-up thing she had done all year. Kieran called Jake «scared» for not going to the big street fight at the rec; that fight ended with the police, while Jake was at home watching TV. In PE Jake was so angry that he stormed off to the changing rooms instead of throwing the ball at Freddie; the PE teacher followed him, said leaving was better than hurting someone, and they talked. When a boy kept pulling Lily's ponytail, she walked to the other side of the playground, and it stopped properly when the teacher on duty noticed and had a word with him.
explain: Walking away does not need a big speech. When a mate dared Sam to go and «sort out» a kid from the other street, Sam said «nah, I've got Lego to finish». His mate laughed and came round to help with the Lego. And when Maddie wanted to stay and argue with the Station Road girls, Sam just said «come on, it's tea time», and they had fish fingers instead.
fact: A fight needs two people, so if one walks away the fight cannot happen.
fact: Walking away is hardest for people who are used to fighting, which is why it counts so much.
fact: Being called «chicken» or «scared» costs nothing; a fight can cost injuries, punishments and police visits.
fact: The best direction to walk away is towards other people, lights, shops and adults.
fact: Shopkeepers, teachers on duty and other grown-ups can help children who feel followed or threatened.
fact: Counting slowly and leaving the room are real ways to let anger cool down.
fact: Not reacting can make a teasing game boring; telling an adult stops it properly.
fact: A joke or an everyday reason, like tea time, can be an easy way out of an argument.
fact: Walking away from someone bigger and stronger is smart, not soft.
fact: Bystanders help most by not cheering and by walking away too.
exercise: When the Station Road boy said «go on then, hit me», what did Sam feel? | A burning in his chest, because leaving looked like losing | Pure joy, because he loves arguing | Nothing at all, because he was not really listening to the boy | Sleepy, because it was very late
exercise: What happened after Sam walked away? | The boy shouted «chicken» a few times, then went home | The boy chased Sam all the way to school | Sam's mates heard about it and stopped speaking to him for a year | The boy came back the next day with his brother
exercise: Why could there be no fight once Sam walked away? | A fight needs two people | The boy was not allowed out | It suddenly started raining | Sam ran much faster than him
exercise: Jake heard a boy insult his football team. What did he do for the first time ever? | Counted to ten and walked away | Threw his fizzy drink at the boy | Told the boy his team was worse | Called his big brother over to help
exercise: Why does Jake walking away count for so much? | Because walking away is hardest for someone who usually fights | Because Jake is the fastest runner in the school and got away easily | Because the boy was very small | Because his team had won that day
exercise: An older girl pushed Maddie at the park. Maddie remembered her sore nose and walked home. How did she feel? | Annoyed with herself and proud at the same time | Only scared and nothing else | Bored, because nothing happened | Sorry for the older girl, who had no friends at the park with her
exercise: What did Maddie's mum say about it? | It was the most grown-up thing Maddie had done all year | Maddie should have pushed her back | Maddie should never go to that park again without her mum and dad | It was not a big deal at all
exercise: Older boys were following Sam and Freddie. Where did they go, and why was it wise? | Into the newsagent's, towards an adult and other people | Into a quiet alley, where nobody could see them | Into the woods, to hide behind trees | Home by the longest and emptiest route, so nobody would see them go
exercise: Kieran called Jake «scared» for not going to the big street fight at the rec. What happened in the end? | The fight ended with the police, and Jake was at home watching TV | Jake's team won and he was a hero | Nothing happened at the rec, because nobody turned up for it in the end | Kieran stopped being friends with everyone
exercise: What is the lesson of Jake and the street fight? | Being called scared is cheaper than a police visit | You must always go if you are dared | Street fights are safe enough, as long as lots of people go | Only grown-ups ever get into trouble
exercise: A boy pulled Lily's ponytail. She walked to the other side of the playground without turning round. What made it stop properly? | The teacher on duty noticed and had a word with him | Lily pulled his hair back the next day so he would know how it felt | The boy forgot about Lily for ever | Lily's ponytail was cut off
exercise: How did Sam get out of the dare to «sort out» a kid from the other street? | He made a joke about finishing his Lego | He gave a long speech about how violence is wrong | He pretended to faint | He ran and hid in the toilets
exercise: In PE Jake was so angry he stormed off to the changing rooms instead of throwing the ball at Freddie. Why was that good? | Leaving to cool off was much better than hurting Freddie | Changing rooms are warmer than the hall | PE was nearly over anyway | Freddie deserved to be left alone in PE after what he had said
exercise: What did the PE teacher do after Jake stormed off? | Followed him, said leaving was better, and they talked | Gave him a detention for leaving | Told the whole class that Jake was a quitter who always gives up | Ignored him for the rest of the week
exercise: Maddie wanted to stay and argue with the Station Road girls. What did Sam give her? | An easy way out: «come on, it's tea time» | A long talk about being polite to everybody | A dare to shout louder at them | A little push towards the girls
exercise: Which of these is ALSO a kind of courage? | Walking away when everyone is shouting «go on» | Hitting the first person who annoys you | Jumping into every argument | Never telling a grown-up anything about what goes on in the street
test: «he said go on hit me, so I did, it's his fault innit». Is that right? | No, nobody has to hit just because they are told to | Yes, he asked for it | Yes, if he says it twice then it's his own fault | Only if he is bigger than you
test: «oi chicken, you just gonna walk off??» What does a wise kid do? | Keep walking; names don't hurt like a fight | Turn back and fight him, just to prove you're not | Shout a worse name back | Stand still and wait for him
test: Two big lads following you and your mate down the high street, it's getting dark. Best move? | Walk calmly into a busy shop and tell the adult there | Run down the nearest empty alley | Stop and ask them straight out what their problem is | Split up so one of you escapes
test: «big fight at the rec 6 o'clock, everyone's going, don't be a wimp». Best answer? | Not going; I'll stay home | Fine, but I'll only stand and watch | I'll go and film it on my phone | I'll bring my brother to help
test: Teacher: «Jake, why did you leave PE without asking?» Best honest answer? | I was really angry and needed to cool off before I did something stupid | I was bored of the game | Freddie told me to go and get out of the hall or he would tell on me | I needed the toilet
test: Lily's ponytail gets yanked AGAIN. Her friend says «just smack him». What's wiser? | Walk away and tell the duty teacher | Smack him hard so he learns | Cut her hair short | Pull his hair back twice as hard
test: «mate dared me to go sort out that kid from Station Road, gonna do it». What's a good way out? | Say no with a joke, like having something better to do | Go, but only shove him a little bit so he doesn't get hurt | Get someone else to do it | Do it, but tell nobody
test: Jake's fists itch and his face goes hot after an insult. What helps most? | Count to ten and turn round | Tense your fists even harder | Stare at the boy until he looks away | Shout the insult back louder
test: Maddie: «If I walk off, they'll say I'm soft». What's true? | Walking away from a bigger person is smart, not soft | She has to fight to keep her name in the playground | She should wait for them to hit first | Only boys can walk away
test: Your friend walked away from a fight everyone was egging on. What can you do? | Say «nice one» and walk with them | Call them chicken with the others | Tell everyone they were scared | Start the fight for them
test: «just ignore him» — mate says. Lily ignored the tugging boy. What stops it for good if ignoring fails? | Telling a grown-up | Ignoring him for another year | Giving him a sweet to share | Moving to a new school
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a confrontation cannot proceed once one party withdraws. | A fight necessarily requires the participation of two persons | The withdrawing party is automatically declared the victor of the dispute | The remaining party is obliged by law to depart | Withdrawal is invariably followed by immediate punishment
exam: It is given that two pupils are pursued by older youths along a public street. Select the course of action most consistent with their safety. | Proceeding calmly to an occupied shop and informing the adult present | Proceeding into an unlit and unoccupied passage | Confronting the pursuers in the street in order to ascertain their intentions | Separating so as to divide the pursuers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the act of withdrawing from a provocation? | It constitutes a form of courage, being more difficult than retaliation | It constitutes an admission of guilt on the part of the pupil who withdraws | It is appropriate only for pupils of small stature | It invariably results in the provocation being repeated
exam: Whereas a pupil is described as «scared» for declining to attend an arranged street fight, ascertain the comparative cost of declining. | The cost of an insult is negligible relative to injury, punishment or police involvement | The cost of declining exceeds any consequence which might follow from attending | Declining is penalised more severely than fighting | No consequence attends either course of action
